    "doi": "https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.18888418",
    "abstract": "Transformative change in cities, landscapes and regions is typically anticipated through computational simulation modelling of future land use scenarios based on estimated land-use change trajectories. One of the greatest sources of uncertainty in such models is the amount of land which is expected to change (land use demand), yet this is significantly underexplored. Modellers often simply fix it at a plausible estimate, e.g. from population tendency projections or policy targets, with only slight variations across very different hypothetical futures. We propose an innovative approach to address this problem. We simulate extreme land-use demand variability in future forest land allocation in Scotland, UK, using a stochastic Markov chain known as a \u201cdrunkard\u2019s walk\u201d, coupled to a cellular automata land use model. The model reveals problematic assumptions in Scotland\u2019s Forestry Strategy, which anticipates 21% of Scotland\u2019s land area under Forestry by 2032. While the area target seems achievable, the timescale is doubtful, since only very extreme variations in the Markov function (5% year on year) allow it to be met by the specified dates, and then only <25% of the time. At the same time, simulations under which the target is met produced runaway afforestation booms leading to loss of natural areas and arable land. Outcomes like these, the chain of events which lead to them, and the timescales on which they may occur, are not apparent from incremental land use demand models or fixed demand scenarios. Our approach opens up new pathways for broader, more disruptive scenarios of land use change.",
    "field": "Land use change modelling",
    "problem": "Existing computational land use models underexplore the significant uncertainty in land use demand, often using fixed or slightly varied estimates which fail to anticipate the potential for extreme variability and disruptive, real-world outcomes such as policy failure or runaway land conversion.",
    "method": "This paper proposes a method that couples a stochastic Markov chain, known as a \u201cdrunkard's walk,\u201d with a cellular automata land use model to simulate extreme variability in land use demand for future forest allocation scenarios.",
    "task": "The task is to simulate extreme land-use demand variability for future forest allocation in Scotland using a stochastic Markov chain coupled to a cellular automata land use model to assess the feasibility of national forestry policy targets.",
