    "innovation": "This paper introduces a stochastic Markov chain model to simulate extreme variability in land use demand, revealing how this approach uncovers disruptive scenarios and assesses the feasibility of policy targets for forest expansion in Scotland.",
    "kg_extraction_triplets": [
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "integrated land use simulation model",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SOURCE_COMPONENT",
            "target_entity_instance": "SIMLANDER model",
            "target_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "paragraph": "To address these research questions, we developed an integrated land use simulation model with two key components; 1) a forest land allocation model (Section 2.1, below), which assigned new forest planting to the most suitable locations, and 2) a stochastic forest demand model (Section 2.2, below) which determined how much forest land should be allocated on the basis of a nature-like evolutionary process chain, where the demand depends only on the previous demand, modified by a stochastic variance term. ... To model forest land use allocation, we adapted the SIMLANDER model (Hewitt et al 2013).",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "integrated land use simulation model",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SOURCE_COMPONENT",
            "target_entity_instance": "stochastic forest demand model",
            "target_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "paragraph": "To address these research questions, we developed an integrated land use simulation model with two key components; 1) a forest land allocation model (Section 2.1, below), which assigned new forest planting to the most suitable locations, and 2) a stochastic forest demand model (Section 2.2, below) which determined how much forest land should be allocated on the basis of a nature-like evolutionary process chain, where the demand depends only on the previous demand, modified by a stochastic variance term.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "SIMLANDER model",
            "source_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "relation": "HAS_PHYSICAL_FEATURE",
            "target_entity_instance": "200m x 200m raster cell size",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalFeature",
            "paragraph": "As a compromise between landscape detail and computational power, a 200m x 200m raster cell size was adopted for all layers.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "SIMLANDER model",
            "source_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "neighbourhood weights",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "To run the model, neighbourhood weights are applied to the starting map of land use (2015) through a moving window filter such that non-forest cells close to existing forest cells are given a higher potential to change to forest land in the next time step than non-forest cells at more distant locations.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "SIMLANDER model",
            "source_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "random variable term",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "A random variable term v is added to the model to ensure that simulations are non-identical, and that unexpected development may occasionally occur. The random factor follows a right-skewed distribution based on the random uniform distribution with most values clustered around 1, such that the stochastic factor mostly has little impact.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": false
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "SIMLANDER model",
            "source_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "relation": "HAS_PHYSICAL_FEATURE",
            "target_entity_instance": "zoning parameter",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalFeature",
            "paragraph": "Finally. areas where forest plantation is impossible or not permitted are included in the zoning parameter. At each time step t+1, neighbourhood (N), randomness (v), suitability (S) and zoning (Z) are computed to produce a total transition potential layer (TTP), P t+1 ij in Eqn. 1, as follows.",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": false,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "stochastic forest demand model",
            "source_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "Markov Chain",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "To solve this problem, we instead determine the land use demand for forestry year-on-year on the basis of a simulated process chain which follows a stochastic pattern of variability. Such a pattern of variability could be considered analogous to a market-based instrument such as a carbon price, a subsidy or certification scheme for land use conversion, or some other form of incentive, such as a policy directive. In our case we use a Markov Chain, with annual demand determined solely by incremental stochastic variation on the previous year (Eqn. 2).",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "stochastic forest demand model",
            "source_entity_type": "SourceComponent",
            "relation": "USES_PHYSICAL_SCHEME",
            "target_entity_instance": "random walk",
            "target_entity_type": "PhysicalScheme",
            "paragraph": "Formally, we model land use demand, \u2206n, as a random walk:\n\u2206n= \u2206n\u22121\u00b7 Xn, n \u2265 1\nWhere Xn is a percentage annual adjustment, set at 100% at when the model is initiated, and calculated thereafter by the sum of the previous value of Xn, Xn-1 and the annual stochastic adjustment, which takes, with equal probability (Pr), a value of +1 or -1 (Eqn 3)",
            "ontology_conformed": false,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        },
        {
            "source_entity_instance": "integrated land use simulation model",
            "source_entity_type": "Source",
            "relation": "HAS_SIMULATION_TYPE",
            "target_entity_instance": "Monte Carlo simulation",
            "target_entity_type": "SimulationType",
            "paragraph": "By using this function to determine land use demand at each time step and then allocating this demand as described above in the land allocation model, we were able to explore, through a multiple run Monte Carlo simulation, the extent of the variation possible in our study case.",
            "ontology_conformed": true,
            "evidence_validated": true,
            "valid": true,
            "eval": true
        }
    ],
